// tests/kernel/mm/tlb_shootdown_race.pdx — R18-M5-004 / #778
// TLB shootdown regression fixture — BSP-side witness (option A).
//
// Issue: paideia-os/paideia-os#778
//
// ============================================================================
// Posture at R18.M5
// ============================================================================
//
// The AC calls for a two-CPU race: one task on core X repeatedly mmaps +
// munmaps a page while another task on core Y reads a fixed address in
// that page. A stale TLB entry surviving the munmap would produce a
// non-faulting read of freed memory; the assertion is "no corruption".
//
// That runtime witness is blocked at HEAD (e01b177) on two upstream
// preconditions:
//
//   1. AP-side IDT install. R18.M1 (#762/#764) brings APs to a
//      cli;hlt loop with no IDT loaded. An inbound TLB shootdown IPI
//      (vec 0xF2, per #775) on an AP #GPs before reaching
//      `_ipi_handler_f2` and the ack is never published — every
//      shootdown from the BSP times out. Fixing this is a peer issue
//      (post-#766 follow-up) and rewriting the fixture around it
//      would duplicate scaffolding once that lands.
//
//   2. Cross-CPU user-mode task execution. Even with APs interrupt-
//      responsive, running mmap/munmap on core X while a reader runs
//      on core Y needs the per-CPU runqueue + AP scheduler bring-up
//      (#773/#774) to actually PICK a user task on the AP; today an
//      AP has no scheduler tick, no idle task, and no `sched_pick_next`
//      wired up. R18.M4 landed the machinery but nothing enqueues
//      user work onto an AP-local runqueue yet.
//
// This fixture therefore lands the BSP-local shape of the witness —
// mechanism + expected-outcome check + deterministic UART marker —
// matching the R18.M5 discipline established by #775 (initiator API
// landed, aspace_unmap integration deferred) and #777 (per-CPU timer
// arming landed, delta-observation witness deferred).
//
// ============================================================================
// What this witness DOES prove (when invoked)
// ============================================================================
//
// `tlb_shootdown_witness` calls `tlb_shootdown_range(va_start, va_end)`
// once with a small 1-page range and asserts the return value equals
// `TLB_SHOOTDOWN_TIMEOUT` (=1). Success means:
//
//   (a) The initiator loop populates every peer's per-CPU CB
//       (SHOOTDOWN_START / SHOOTDOWN_END / SHOOTDOWN_EPOCH at CB[+88 /
//       +96 / +104]) without corrupting adjacent fields — otherwise the
//       following `lapic_send_ipi_broadcast_excl_self` or the peer-poll
//       loop would trip a fault or spin on wrong slots.
//
//   (b) The local-INVLPG sweep walks the requested range with the
//       page-step and budget cap intact — a bug in the walker would
//       either hang (missing bound check) or #GP on a bogus VA
//       (mis-encoded `invlpg [rbx]`).
//
//   (c) The broadcast IPI helper (`lapic_send_ipi_broadcast_excl_self`,
//       #771) accepts vec 0xF2 and returns without asserting on ICR
//       delivery — proves the LAPIC ICR path is wire-correct even
//       though the receivers cannot ack.
//
//   (d) The peer-ack poll respects `ACK_POLL_BUDGET` and returns
//       `TLB_SHOOTDOWN_TIMEOUT` rather than hanging — the bounded-poll
//       is the safety net that lets the initiator return under
//       AP-not-yet-responsive conditions (SDM Vol 3A §4.10.4 leaves
//       receiver latency unspecified; our budget is the practical
//       upper bound observed on TCG).
//
//   (e) The mfence discipline (#776) around the CB writes + IPI + ack
//       loop composes correctly with real callee-save state on a live
//       call from kernel_main.
//
// ============================================================================
// What this witness does NOT prove (deferred)
// ============================================================================
//
// - Peer receiver actually invalidates the range (needs #762/#766
//   follow-up: AP-side IDT install so `_ipi_handler_f2` runs on the
//   receiver and publishes its ack).
// - No stale-TLB corruption under concurrent mmap+munmap on core X and
//   read on core Y (needs (1) receiver-side ack from above, plus (2)
//   per-CPU runqueue user-task enqueue on APs via R18.M4-plus).
// - The 100M-iteration ACK_POLL_BUDGET is not the wrong ceiling for
//   real hardware — a hardware-realistic ceiling requires per-peer
//   latency measurement once APs can ack. The current budget is TCG-
//   sized (a busy poll of ~600ms-3s at TCG speeds; on real silicon
//   the whole rendezvous is single-digit microseconds).
//
// ============================================================================
// Wire-in status
// ============================================================================
//
// This module DEFINES the witness function but the invocation from
// `kernel_main.boot_continue_after_ring3` is deliberately DEFERRED.
// The reason is timing: with no AP able to ack (see blocker (1) above),
// every invocation exhausts the 100M poll budget per peer. On TCG that
// is ~600ms-3s per full 3-peer round — comfortably under the boot_smp
// smoke's 10s TIMEOUT, but over the 5s budget of boot_min /
// boot_banner / boot_tick / boot_r8_only / boot_r14b_hivma /
// boot_r14b_kpti / boot_r14b_ipi / boot_r14b_ud. Wiring the call
// unconditionally would either regress those smokes or require
// bumping their TIMEOUTs, both of which are out of scope for a
// fixture landing.
//
// The follow-up that flips the invocation on is one of:
//   (i)   AP IDT install (#762/#766 follow-up) — receivers ack in
//         microseconds, so the whole call is microseconds and safe
//         to invoke from every mode.
//   (ii)  Per-call `ack_budget` parameter on `tlb_shootdown_range`
//         (a #775 follow-up) — the witness passes a small budget
//         (~1M) that bounds the failure-path spin regardless of
//         receiver state.
//   (iii) Mode-gated boot flow that only invokes the witness under
//         boot_smp (needs a compile-time or CB-observable flag
//         that other smokes can skip). Least preferred — adds
//         mode-dispatch complexity to the boot path.
//
// Once one of these lands, the invocation site in kernel_main.pdx is
// a one-liner: `call tlb_shootdown_witness;` between the SMP BRINGUP
// DONE emit (~L8844) and the init bootstrap (~L8847).
//
// ============================================================================
// UART markers
// ============================================================================
//
//   Success: "TLB SHOOTDOWN WITNESS OK\n"    (25 + \n + \0 = 27 bytes)
//   Failure: "TLB SHOOTDOWN WITNESS FAIL\n"  (26 + \n + \0 = 28 bytes)
//
// Emitted via raw `uart_puts` (not klog_s1) so the bytes hit COM1
// without a level/subsystem prefix — matches the `SMP BRINGUP START /
// DONE` idiom (kernel_main.pdx L9070-9071). Trailing `\n` gives the
// fingerprint checker a line boundary; trailing `\0` terminates the
// `uart_puts` char-copy loop.
//
// ============================================================================
// Encoder
// ============================================================================
//
// paideia-as v0.20.1+. Uses call, ret, mov (imm64 + reg-reg), cmp,
// je, jmp, lea (rip-relative), and sub/add rsp for SysV 16-byte
// alignment across nested `call`. No new encoder work.

module TlbShootdownRace = structure {

  // UART markers (see header §UART markers).
  pub let tlb_shootdown_witness_ok_msg   : [u8; 27] = "TLB SHOOTDOWN WITNESS OK\n\0"
  pub let tlb_shootdown_witness_fail_msg : [u8; 28] = "TLB SHOOTDOWN WITNESS FAIL\n\0"

  // TLB_SHOOTDOWN_TIMEOUT sentinel — mirror of the constant in
  // core/mm/tlb_shootdown.pdx (kept as a local re-declaration because
  // paideia-as modules do not currently import cross-file `pub let`
  // constants without a call-site round-trip).
  let TLB_SHOOTDOWN_TIMEOUT : u64 = 1

  // Witness VA range: 1 page starting at 0x1000 (low canonical space,
  // deliberately unmapped in the kernel CR3). `invlpg` on an unmapped
  // VA is a no-op per SDM Vol 2B §INVLPG ("If no TLB entry corresponds
  // to the specified address, INVLPG has no effect (however, no
  // exception is generated)") — so the local-sweep step exercises the
  // walker without perturbing any live mapping.
  let WITNESS_VA_START : u64 = 0x1000
  let WITNESS_VA_END   : u64 = 0x2000

  // ==========================================================================
  // tlb_shootdown_witness() -> ()
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Body:
  //   (1) Call tlb_shootdown_range(WITNESS_VA_START, WITNESS_VA_END).
  //   (2) Compare rax to TLB_SHOOTDOWN_TIMEOUT (=1).
  //   (3) Match  → uart_puts(tlb_shootdown_witness_ok_msg).
  //       Miss   → uart_puts(tlb_shootdown_witness_fail_msg).
  //   (4) Return.
  //
  // Register discipline (SysV AMD64):
  //   No callee-save regs touched — every value crosses at most one
  //   `call` and is re-materialised via `lea [rip + ...]` / `mov imm64`
  //   before the next call. Single `sub rsp, 8` / `add rsp, 8` bracket
  //   keeps the stack 16-byte aligned across the nested calls (function
  //   entry has rsp % 16 == 8 from the caller's `call`; the sub 8 lands
  //   us at rsp % 16 == 0 for the nested calls).
  //
  // Effect / capability rows:
  //   {sysreg, mem} — tlb_shootdown_range writes to peer CBs and
  //   issues rdmsr; uart_puts touches COM1 MMIO/IO.
  //   {boot} — this witness is a bring-up structural check, not a
  //   runtime primitive; the same capability tier as
  //   tlb_shootdown_range and gs_base_init_bsp.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let tlb_shootdown_witness : () -> () !{sysreg, mem} @{boot} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: { sysreg, mem },
      capabilities: { boot },
      justification: "#778 R18-M5-004: BSP-side TLB shootdown witness. Calls tlb_shootdown_range(0x1000, 0x2000) — a 1-page range in low canonical space, deliberately unmapped so the initiator's local INVLPG loop exercises the walker without perturbing live mappings (SDM Vol 2B §INVLPG guarantees no-op on unmapped VA, no #GP). Expected return: TLB_SHOOTDOWN_TIMEOUT (=1) because APs park in cli;hlt without an IDT (R18.M1 posture — see #762/#766 follow-up); receivers therefore cannot service vec 0xF2 and cannot publish an ack. On match: emit `TLB SHOOTDOWN WITNESS OK\\n` via raw uart_puts (same idiom as smp_bringup_start_msg / smp_bringup_done_msg — bypasses klog_s1 level/subsystem prefix). On miss: emit `TLB SHOOTDOWN WITNESS FAIL\\n`. Wire-in from kernel_main is DEFERRED (this witness is defined but not called at HEAD) — see header §Wire-in status for the timing rationale (poll-budget exhaustion at TCG speeds blows 5s TIMEOUT smokes without a per-call budget knob or AP IDT install). Register discipline: no callee-save touched; single sub rsp,8 / add rsp,8 bracket keeps rsp % 16 == 0 at each nested call boundary (function entry rsp % 16 == 8 → -8 = 16-aligned). Effect row {sysreg, mem} matches tlb_shootdown_range's row plus uart_puts's port I/O. {boot} capability restricts callers to bring-up code, mirroring tlb_shootdown_range and gs_base_init_bsp.",
      block: {
        sub rsp, 8;                              // align: rsp % 16 == 8 on entry, -8 = 0

        // (1) call tlb_shootdown_range(WITNESS_VA_START, WITNESS_VA_END)
        mov rdi, 0x1000;                         // WITNESS_VA_START
        mov rsi, 0x2000;                         // WITNESS_VA_END
        call tlb_shootdown_range;

        // (2) compare rax to TLB_SHOOTDOWN_TIMEOUT (=1)
        cmp rax, 1;
        jne witness_fail;

        // (3a) match → emit OK
        lea rdi, [rip + tlb_shootdown_witness_ok_msg];
        call uart_puts;
        jmp witness_epilogue;

        // (3b) miss → emit FAIL
        witness_fail:
        lea rdi, [rip + tlb_shootdown_witness_fail_msg];
        call uart_puts;

        // (4) epilogue
        witness_epilogue:
        add rsp, 8;
        ret
      }
    }
}
